:: 0.11.0\n   135→\n   136→    \"\"\"\n   137→\n   138→    error_template = 'Found `else` in a loop without `break`'\n   139→    code = 500\n   140→    previous_codes = {436}\n   141→\n   142→\n   143→@final\n   144→class UselessFinallyViolation(ASTViolation):\n   145→    \"\"\"\n   146→    Forbid ``finally`` in ``try`` block without ``except`` block.\n   147→\n   148→    However, we allow to use ``try`` with just ``finally`` block\n   149→    when function or method is decorated. Because we cannot control\n   150→    what is going on in this decorator.\n   151→    It might be ``@contextmanager`` or similar thing that requires this API.\n   152→\n   153→    Reasoning:\n   154→        This rule will reduce complexity and improve readability.\n   155→\n   156→    Solution:\n   157→        Refactor your ``try`` logic.\n   158→        Replace the ``try-finally`` statement with a ``with`` statement.\n   159→\n   160→    Example::\n   161→\n   162→        # Correct:\n   163→        with open(\"filename\") as f:\n   164→            f.write(...)\n   165→\n   166→        # Wrong:\n   167→        try:\n   168→            f = open(\"filename\")\n   169→            f.write(...)\n   170→        finally:\n   171→            f.close()\n   172→\n   173→    .. versionadded:: 0.3.0\n   174→    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n   175→    .. versionchanged:: 0.14.0\n   176→\n   177→    \"\"\"\n   178→\n   179→    error_template = 'Found `finally` in `try` block without `except`'\n   180→    code = 501\n   181→    previous_codes = {437}\n   182→\n   183→\n   184→@final\n   185→class SimplifiableIfViolation(ASTViolation):\n   186→    \"\"\"\n   187→    Forbid simplifiable ``if`` conditions.\n   188→\n   189→    Reasoning:\n   190→        These complex constructions can cause frustration among other\n   191→        developers. They are longer, more verbose, and more complex.\n   192→\n   193→    Solution:\n   194→        Either use ``bool()`` to convert test values to boolean values, or just\n   195→        leave it as it is in case your test already returns a boolean value.\n   196→        Use can also use ``not`` keyword to switch boolean values.\n   197→\n   198→    Example::\n   199→\n   200→        # Correct:\n   201→        my_bool = bool(some_call())\n   202→        other_value = 8 if some_call() else None\n   203→\n   204→        # Wrong:\n   205→        my_bool = True if some_call() else False\n   206→\n   207→    We only check ``if`` nodes where ``True`` and ``False`` values are used.\n   208→    We check both ``if`` nodes and ``if`` expressions.\n   209→\n   210→    .. versionadded:: 0.7.0\n   211→    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n   212→\n   213→    \"\"\"\n   214→\n   215→    error_template = 'Found simplifiable `if` condition'\n   216→    code = 502\n   217→    previous_codes = {451}\n   218→\n   219→\n   220→@final\n   221→class UselessReturningElseViolation(ASTViolation):\n   222→    \"\"\"\n   223→    Forbid useless ``else`` cases in returning functions.\n   224→\n   225→    We check single ``if``, ``for``, ``while``, and ``try``\n   226→    statements that all contain\n   227→    ``return``, ``raise``, ``continue``, or ``break``\n   228→    statements with this rule.\n   229→\n   230→    Reasoning:\n   231→        Using extra ``else`` creates a situation when\n   232→        the whole node could and should be dropped\n   233→        without any changes in logic.\n   234→        So, we prefer to have less code than more code.\n   235→\n   236→    Solution:\n   237→        Remove useless ``else`` case.\n   238→\n   239→    Example::\n   240→\n   241→        # Correct:\n   242→        def some_function():\n   243→            if some_call():\n   244→                return 'yeap'\n   245→            return 'nope'\n   246→\n   247→        # Wrong:\n   248→        def some_function():\n   249→            if some_call():\n   250→                raise ValueError('yeap')\n   251→            else:\n   252→                raise ValueError('nope')\n   253→\n   254→    .. versionadded:: 0.7.0\n   255→    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n   256→    .. versionchanged:: 0.15.1\n   257→\n   258→    \"\"\"\n   259→\n   260→    error_template = 'Found useless returning `else` statement'\n   261→    code = 503\n   262→    previous_codes = {457}\n   263→\n   264→\n   265→@final\n   266→class NegatedConditionsViolation(ASTViolation):\n   267→    \"\"\"\n   268→    Forbid negated conditions together with ``else`` clause.\n   269→\n   270→    Reasoning:\n   271→        It easier to read and name regular conditions. Not negated ones.\n   272→\n   273→    Solution:\n   274→        Move actions from the negated ``if`` condition to the ``else``\n   275→        condition.\n   276→\n   277→    Example::\n   278→\n   279→        # Correct:\n   280→        if some == 1:\n   281→             ...\n   282→        else:\n   283→             ...\n   284→\n   285→        if not some:\n   286→             ...\n   287→\n   288→        if not some:\n   289→            ...\n   290→        elif other:\n   291→            ...\n   292→\n   293→        # Wrong:\n   294→        if not some:\n   295→             ...\n   296→        else:\n   297→             ...\n   298→\n   299→    .. versionadded:: 0.8.0\n   300→    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n   301→\n   302→    \"\"\"\n   303→\n   304→    error_template = 'Found negated condition'\n   305→    code = 504\n   306→    previous_codes = {463}\n   307→\n   308→\n   309→@final\n   310→class NestedTryViolation(ASTViolation):\n   311→    \"\"\"\n   312→    Forbid nested ``try`` blocks.\n   313→\n   314→    Notice, we check all possible slots for ``try`` block:\n   315→    1. the ``try`` block itself\n   316→    2. all ``except`` cases\n   317→    3. ``else`` case\n   318→    4. and ``finally`` case\n   319→\n   320→    Reasoning:\n   321→        Nesting ``try`` blocks indicates\n   322→        that something really bad happens to your logic.\n   323→        Why does it require two separate exception handlers?\n   324→        It is a perfect case to refactor your code.\n   325→\n   326→    Solution:\n   327→        Collapse two exception handlers together.\n   328→        Or create a separate function that will handle this second nested case.\n   329→\n   330→    Example::\n   331→\n   332→        # Wrong:\n   333→        try:\n   334→            try:\n   335→                ...\n   336→            except SomeException:\n   337→                ...\n   338→        except SomeOtherException:\n   339→            ...\n   340→\n   341→        try:\n   342→            ...\n   343→        except SomeOtherException:\n   344→            try:\n   345→                ...\n   346→            except SomeException:\n   347→                ...\n   348→\n   349→    .. versionadded:: 0.8.0\n   350→    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n   351→\n   352→    \"\"\"\n   353→\n   354→    error_template = 'Found nested `try` block'\n   355→    code = 505\n   356→    previous_codes = {464}\n   357→\n   358→\n   359→@final\n   360→class UselessLambdaViolation(ASTViolation):\n   361→    \"\"\"\n   362→    Forbid useless proxy ``lambda`` expressions.\n   363→\n   364→    Reasoning:\n   365→        Sometimes developers tend to overuse ``lambda`` expressions\n   366→        and they wrap code that can be passed as is, without extra wrapping.\n   367→        The code without extra ``lambda`` is easier\n   368→        to read and is more performant.\n   369→\n   370→    Solution:\n   371→        Remove wrapping ``lambda`` declaration, use just the internal function.\n   372→\n   373→    Example::\n   374→\n   375→        # Correct:\n   376→        numbers = map(int, ['1', '2'])\n   377→\n   378→        # Wrong:\n   379→        numbers = map(lambda string: int(string), ['1', '2'])\n   380→\n   381→    .. versionadded:: 0.10.0\n   382→    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n   383→\n   384→    \"\"\"\n   385→\n   386→    error_template = 'Found useless lambda declaration'\n   387→    code = 506\n   388→    previous_codes = {467}\n   389→\n   390→\n   391→@final\n   392→class UselessLenCompareViolation(ASTViolation):\n   393→    \"\"\"\n   394→    Forbid unpythonic zero-length compare.\n   395→\n   396→    Note, that we allow to check arbitrary length, like ``len(arr) == 3``.\n   397→\n   398→    Reasoning:\n   399→        Python's structures like dicts, lists, sets, and tuples\n   400→        all have ``__bool__`` method to checks their length.\n   401→        So, there's no point in wrapping them into ``len(...)``\n   402→        and checking that it is bigger that ``0`` or less then ``1``, etc.\n   403→\n   404→    Solution:\n   405→        Remove extra ``len()`` call.\n   406→\n   407→    Example::\n   408→\n   409→        # Correct:\n   410→        if some_array or not other_array or len(third_array) == 1:\n   411→            ...\n   412→\n   413→        # Wrong:\n   414→        if len(some_array) > 0 or len(other_array) < 1:\n   415→            ...\n   416→\n   417→    .. versionadded:: 0.10.0\n   418→    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n   419→\n   420→    \"\"\"\n   421→\n   422→    error_template = 'Found useless `len()` compare'\n   423→    code = 507\n   424→    previous_codes = {468}\n   425→\n   426→\n   427→@final\n   428→class NotOperatorWithCompareViolation(ASTViolation):\n   429→    \"\"\"\n   430→    Forbid ``not`` with compare expressions.\n   431→\n   432→    Reasoning:\n   433→        This version of ``not`` operator is unreadable.\n   434→\n   435→    Solution:\n   436→        Refactor the expression without ``not`` operator.\n   437→        Change the compare signs.\n   438→\n   439→    Example::\n   440→\n   441→        # Correct:\n   442→        if x <= 5:\n   443→            ...\n   444→\n   445→        # Wrong:\n   446→        if not x > 5:\n   447→            ...\n   448→\n   449→    .. versionadded:: 0.10.0\n   450→    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n   451→\n   452→    \"\"\"\n   453→\n   454→    error_template = 'Found incorrect `not` with compare usage'\n   455→    code = 508\n   456→    previous_codes = {470}\n   457→\n   458→\n   459→@final\n   460→class NestedTernaryViolation(ASTViolation):\n   461→    \"\"\"\n   462→    Forbid nesting ternary expressions in certain places.\n   463→\n   464→    Note, that we restrict to nest ternary expressions inside:\n   465→\n   466→    - ``if`` conditions\n   467→    - boolean and binary operations like ``and`` or ``+``\n   468→    - unary operators\n   469→\n   470→    Reasoning:\n   471→        Nesting ternary in random places can lead to very hard\n   472→        debug and testing problems.\n   473→\n   474→    Solution:\n   475→        Refactor the ternary expression to be either a new variable,\n   476→        or nested ``if`` statement, or a new function.\n   477→\n   478→    Example::\n   479→\n   480→        # Correct:\n   481→        some = x if cond() else y\n   482→\n   483→        # Wrong:\n   484→        if x if cond() else y:\n   485→            ...\n   486→\n   487→    .. versionadded:: 0.10.0\n   488→    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n   489→\n   490→    \"\"\"\n   491→\n   492→    error_template = 'Found incorrectly nested ternary'\n   493→    code = 509\n   494→    previous_codes = {472}\n   495→\n   496→\n   497→@final\n   498→class WrongInCompareTypeViolation(ASTViolation):\n   499→    \"\"\"\n   500→    Forbid ``in`` with static containers except ``set`` nodes.\n   501→\n   502→    We enforce people to use sets as a static containers.\n   503→    You can also use variables, calls, methods, etc.\n   504→    Dynamic values are not checked.\n   505→\n   506→    Reasoning:\n   507→        Using static ``list``, ``tuple``, or ``dict`` elements\n   508→        to check that some element is inside the container is a bad practice.\n   509→        Because we need to iterate all over the container to find the element.\n   510→        Sets are the best suit for this task.\n   511→        Moreover, it makes your code consistent.\n   512→\n   513→    Solution:\n   514→        Use ``set`` elements or comprehensions to check that something\n   515→        is contained in a container.\n   516→\n   517→    Example::\n   518→\n   519→        # Correct:\n   520→        print(needle in {'one', 'two'})\n   521→\n   522→        # Wrong:\n   523→        print(needle in ['one', 'two'])\n   524→\n   525→    .. versionadded:: 0.10.0\n   526→    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n   527→    .. versionchanged:: 0.14.0\n   528→\n   529→    \"\"\"\n   530→\n   531→    error_template = 'Found `in` used with a non-set container'\n   532→    code = 510\n   533→    previous_codes = {473}\n   534→\n   535→\n   536→@final\n   537→class UnmergedIsinstanceCallsViolation(ASTViolation):\n   538→    \"\"\"\n   539→    Forbid multiple ``isinstance`` calls on the same variable.\n   540→\n   541→    Reasoning:\n   542→        The best practice is to use ``isinstance`` with tuple\n   543→        as the second argument, instead of multiple conditions\n   544→        joined with ``or``.\n   545→\n   546→    Solution:\n   547→        Use tuple of types as the second argument.\n   548→\n   549→    Example::\n   550→\n   551→        # Correct:\n   552→        isinstance(some, (int, float))\n   553→\n   554→        # Wrong:\n   555→        isinstance(some, int) or isinstance(some, float)\n   556→\n   557→    See also:\n   558→        https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#isinstance\n   559→\n   560→    .. versionadded:: 0.10.0\n   561→    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n   562→\n   563→    \"\"\"\n   564→\n   565→    error_template = (\n   566→        'Found separate `isinstance` calls that can be merged for: {0}'\n   567→    )\n   568→    code = 511\n   569→    previous_codes = {474}\n   570→\n   571→\n   572→@final\n   573→class WrongIsinstanceWithTupleViolation(ASTViolation):\n   574→    \"\"\"\n   575→    Forbid multiple ``isinstance`` calls with single-item tuples.\n   576→\n   577→    Reasoning:\n   578→        There's no need to use tuples with single elements.\n   579→        You can use single variables or tuples with multiple elements.\n   580→\n   581→    Solution:\n   582→        Use tuples with multiple elements or a single variable.\n   583→\n   584→    Example::\n   585→\n   586→        # Correct:\n   587→        isinstance(some, (int, float))\n   588→        isinstance(some, int)\n   589→\n   590→        # Wrong:\n   591→        isinstance(some, (int, ))\n   592→\n   593→    See: https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#isinstance\n   594→\n   595→    .. versionadded:: 0.10.0\n   596→    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n   597→\n   598→    \"\"\"\n   599→\n   600→    error_template = 'Found `isinstance` call with a single element tuple'\n   601→    code = 512\n   602→    previous_codes = {475}\n   603→\n   604→\n   605→@final\n   606→class ImplicitElifViolation(TokenizeViolation):\n   607→    \"\"\"\n   608→    Forbid implicit ``elif`` conditions.\n   609→\n   610→    Reasoning:\n   611→        Nested ``if`` in ``else`` cases are bad\n   612→        for readability because of the nesting level.\n   613→\n   614→    Solution:\n   615→        Use ``elif`` on the same level.\n   616→\n   617→    Example::\n   618→\n   619→        # Correct:\n   620→        if some:\n   621→            ...\n   622→        elif other:\n   623→            ...\n   624→\n   625→        # Wrong:\n   626→        if some:\n   627→            ...\n   628→        else:\n   629→            if other:\n   630→                ...\n   631→\n   632→    .. versionadded:: 0.12.0\n   633→\n   634→    \"\"\"\n   635→\n   636→    error_template = 'Found implicit `elif` condition'\n   637→    code = 513\n   638→\n   639→\n   640→@final\n   641→class ImplicitInConditionViolation(ASTViolation):\n   642→    \"\"\"\n   643→    Forbid multiple equality comparisons with the same variable.\n   644→\n   645→    Reasoning:\n   646→        Using double+ equality compare with ``or``\n   647→        or double+ non-equality compare with ``and``\n   648→        indicates that you have implicit ``in`` or ``not in`` condition.\n   649→        It is just hidden from you.\n   650→\n   651→    Solution:\n   652→        Refactor compares to use ``in`` or ``not in`` clauses.\n   653→\n   654→\n   655→    Example::\n   656→\n   657→        # Correct:\n   658→        print(some in {'first', 'second'})\n   659→        print(some not in {'first', 'second'})\n   660→\n   661→        # Wrong:\n   662→        print(some == 'first' or some == 'second')\n   663→        print(some != 'first' and some != 'second')\n   664→\n   665→    .. versionadded:: 0.10.0\n   666→    .. versionchanged:: 0.12.0\n   667→\n   668→    \"\"\"\n   669→\n   670→    code = 514\n   671→    error_template = 'Found implicit `in` condition: {0}'\n   672→    previous_codes = {336}\n   673→\n   674→\n   675→@final\n   676→class OpenWithoutContextManagerViolation(ASTViolation):\n   677→    \"\"\"\n   678→    Forbid ``open()`` without a context manager.\n   679→\n   680→    Reasoning:\n   681→        When you ``open()`` something, you need to close it.\n   682→        When using a context manager - it is automatically done for you.\n   683→        When not using it - you might find yourself in a situation\n   684→        when file is not closed and is not accessible anymore.\n   685→\n   686→    Solution:\n   687→        Refactor ``open()`` call to use ``with``.\n   688→\n   689→    Example::\n   690→\n   691→        # Correct:\n   692→        with open(filename) as file_obj:\n   693→            ...\n   694→\n   695→        # Wrong:\n   696→        file_obj = open(filename)\n   697→\n   698→    .. versionadded:: 0.12.0\n   699→\n   700→    \"\"\"\n   701→\n   702→    code = 515\n   703→    error_template = 'Found `open()` used without a context manager'\n   704→\n   705→\n   706→@final\n   707→class TypeCompareViolation(ASTViolation):\n   708→    \"\"\"\n   709→    Forbid comparing types with ``type()`` function.\n   710→\n   711→    Reasoning:\n   712→        When you compare types with ``type()`` function call\n   713→        it means that you break polymorphism and disallow child classes\n   714→        of a node to work here. That's incorrect.\n   715→\n   716→    Solution:\n   717→        Use ``isinstance`` to compare types.\n   718→\n   719→    Example::\n   720→\n   721→        # Correct:\n   722→        print(something, type(something))\n   723→\n   724→        if isinstance(something, int):\n   725→            ...\n   726→\n   727→        # Wrong:\n   728→        if type(something) == int:\n   729→            ...\n   730→\n   731→    .. versionadded:: 0.12.0\n   732→\n   733→    \"\"\"\n   734→\n   735→    code = 516\n   736→    error_template = 'Found `type()` used to compare types'\n   737→\n   738→\n   739→@final\n   740→class PointlessStarredViolation(ASTViolation):\n   741→    \"\"\"\n   742→    Forbid useless starred expressions.\n   743→\n   744→    Reasoning:\n   745→        Using starred expression with constants is useless.\n   746→        This piece of code can be rewritten to be flat.\n   747→        Eg.: ``print(*[1, 2, 3])`` is ``print(1, 2, 3)``.\n   748→\n   749→    Solution:\n   750→        Refactor your code not to use starred expressions\n   751→        with ``list``, ``dict``, ``tuple``, and ``set`` constants.\n   752→        Use regular argument passing instead.\n   753→\n   754→    Example::\n   755→\n   756→        # Correct:\n   757→        my_list = [1, 2, 3, *other_iterable]\n   758→\n   759→        # Wrong:\n   760→        print(*[1, 2, 3], **{{}})\n   761→\n   762→    .. versionadded:: 0.12.0\n   763→\n   764→    \"\"\"\n   765→\n   766→    code = 517\n   767→    error_template = 'Found pointless starred expression'\n   768→\n   769→\n   770→@final\n   771→class ImplicitEnumerateViolation(ASTViolation):\n   772→    \"\"\"\n   773→    Forbid implicit ``enumerate()`` calls.\n   774→\n   775→    Reasoning:\n   776→        Using ``range(len(...))`` is not pythonic.\n   777→        Python uses collection iterators, not index-based loops.\n   778→\n   779→    Solution:\n   780→        Use ``enumerate(...)`` instead of ``range(len(...))``.\n   781→\n   782→    Example::\n   783→\n   784→        # Correct:\n   785→        for index, person in enumerate(people):\n   786→            ...\n   787→\n   788→        # Wrong:\n   789→        for index in range(len(people)):\n   790→            ...\n   791→\n   792→    See also:\n   793→        https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#enumerate\n   794→\n   795→    .. versionadded:: 0.12.0\n   796→\n   797→    \"\"\"\n   798→\n   799→    code = 518\n   800→    error_template = 'Found implicit `enumerate()` call'\n   801→\n   802→\n   803→@final\n   804→class ImplicitSumViolation(ASTViolation):\n   805→    \"\"\"\n   806→    Forbid implicit ``sum()`` calls.\n   807→\n   808→    When summing types different from numbers, you might need to provide\n   809→    the second argument to the ``sum`` function: ``sum([[1], [2], [3]], [])``\n   810→\n   811→    You might also use ``str.join`` to join iterable of strings.\n   812→\n   813→    Reasoning:\n   814→        Using ``for`` loops with ``+=`` assign inside indicates\n   815→        that you iteratively sum things inside your collection.\n   816→        That's what ``sum()`` builtin function does.\n   817→\n   818→    Solution:\n   819→        Use ``sum(...)`` instead of a loop with ``+=`` operation.\n   820→\n   821→    Example::\n   822→\n   823→        # Correct:\n   824→        sum_result = sum(get_elements())\n   825→\n   826→        # Wrong:\n   827→        sum_result = 0\n   828→        for to_sum in get_elements():\n   829→            sum_result += to_sum\n   830→\n   831→    See also:\n   832→        https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#sum\n   833→        https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#str.join\n   834→\n   835→    .. versionadded:: 0.12.0\n   836→\n   837→    \"\"\"\n   838→\n   839→    code = 519\n   840→    error_template = 'Found implicit `sum()` call'\n   841→\n   842→\n   843→@final\n   844→class FalsyConstantCompareViolation(ASTViolation):\n   845→    \"\"\"\n   846→    Forbid comparing with explicit falsy constants.\n   847→\n   848→    We allow to compare with falsy numbers, strings, booleans, ``None``.\n   849→    We disallow complex constants like tuple, dicts, and lists.\n   850→\n   851→    Reasoning:\n   852→        When comparing ``something`` with explicit falsy constants\n   853→        what we really mean is ``not something``.\n   854→\n   855→    Solution:\n   856→        Use ``not`` with your variable.\n   857→        Fix your data types.\n   858→\n   859→    Example::\n   860→\n   861→        # Correct:\n   862→        if not my_check:\n   863→            ...\n   864→\n   865→        if some_other is None:\n   866→            ...\n   867→\n   868→        if some_num == 0:\n   869→            ...\n   870→\n   871→        # Wrong:\n   872→        if my_check == []:\n   873→            ...\n   874→\n   875→    .. versionadded:: 0.12.0\n   876→\n   877→    \"\"\"\n   878→\n   879→    code = 520\n   880→    error_template = 'Found compare with falsy constant'\n   881→\n   882→\n   883→@final\n   884→class WrongIsCompareViolation(ASTViolation):\n   885→    \"\"\"\n   886→    Forbid comparing values with constants using ``is`` or ``is not``.\n   887→\n   888→    However, we allow to compare with ``None`` and booleans.\n   889→\n   890→    Reasoning:\n   891→        ``is`` compares might not do what you want them to do.\n   892→        Firstly, they check for the same object, not equality.\n   893→        Secondly, they behave unexpectedly even\n   894→        with the simple values like ``257``.\n   895→\n   896→    Solution:\n   897→        Use ``==`` to compare with constants.\n   898→\n   899→    Example::\n   900→\n   901→        # Correct:\n   902→        if my_check == [1, 2, 3]:\n   903→            ...\n   904→\n   905→        # Wrong:\n   906→        if my_check is [1, 2, 3]:\n   907→            ...\n   908→\n   909→    See also:\n   910→        https://stackoverflow.com/a/33130014/4842742\n   911→\n   912→    .. versionadded:: 0.12.0\n   913→\n   914→    \"\"\"\n   915→\n   916→    code = 521\n   917→    error_template = 'Found wrong `is` compare'\n   918→\n   919→\n   920→@final\n   921→class ImplicitPrimitiveViolation(ASTViolation):\n   922→    \"\"\"\n   923→    Forbid implicit primitives in the form of ``lambda`` functions.\n   924→\n   925→    Reasoning:\n   926→        When you use ``lambda`` that returns a primitive value\n   927→        and takes no arguments, it means that\n   928→        you should use a primitive type instead.\n   929→\n   930→    Solution:\n   931→        Replace ``lambda`` with ``int``, ``float``,\n   932→        ``list``, or any other primitive.\n   933→\n   934→    Example::\n   935→\n   936→        # Correct:\n   937→        defaultdict(int)\n   938→\n   939→        # Wrong:\n   940→        defaultdict(lambda: 0)\n   941→\n   942→    .. versionadded:: 0.13.0\n   943→\n   944→    \"\"\"\n   945→\n   946→    code = 522\n   947→    error_template = 'Found implicit primitive in a form of `lambda`'\n   948→\n   949→\n   950→@final\n   951→class AlmostSwappedViolation(ASTViolation):\n   952→    \"\"\"\n   953→    Forbid unpythonic variable swaps.\n   954→\n   955→    We check for ``a = b; b = a`` sequences.\n   956→\n   957→    Reasoning:\n   958→        This looks like a failed attempt to swap.\n   959→\n   960→    Solution:\n   961→        Use standard way to swap two variables.\n   962→\n   963→    Example::\n   964→\n   965→        # Correct:\n   966→        a, b = b, a\n   967→\n   968→        # Wrong:\n   969→        a = b\n   970→        b = a\n   971→\n   972→        temp = a\n   973→        a = b\n   974→        b = temp\n   975→\n   976→    .. versionadded:: 0.13.0\n   977→\n   978→    \"\"\"\n   979→\n   980→    error_template = 'Found incorrectly swapped variables'\n   981→    code = 523\n   982→\n   983→\n   984→@final\n   985→class MisrefactoredAssignmentViolation(ASTViolation):\n   986→    \"\"\"\n   987→    Forbid misrefactored self assignment.\n   988→\n   989→    Reasoning:\n   990→        Self assignment does not need to have the same operand\n   991→        on the left hand side and on the right hand side.\n   992→\n   993→    Solution:\n   994→        Refactor your code to use multiple self assignments or fix your code.\n   995→\n   996→    Example::\n   997→\n   998→        # Correct:\n   999→        test += 1\n  1000→        test *= 2\n  1001→\n  1002→        # Wrong:\n  1003→        test += test + 1\n  1004→\n  1005→    See\n  1006→    :py:data:`~wemake_python_styleguide.constants.MATH_APPROXIMATE_CONSTANTS`\n  1007→    for full list of math constants that we check for.\n  1008→\n  1009→    .. versionadded:: 0.13.0\n  1010→\n  1011→    \"\"\"\n  1012→\n  1013→    error_template = 'Found self assignment  with refactored assignment'\n  1014→    code = 524\n  1015→\n  1016→\n  1017→@final\n  1018→class InCompareWithSingleItemContainerViolation(ASTViolation):\n  1019→    \"\"\"\n  1020→    Forbid comparisons where ``in`` is compared with single item container.\n  1021→\n  1022→    Reasoning:\n  1023→        ``in`` comparison with a container which contains only one item looks\n  1024→        like overhead and unneeded complexity.\n  1025→\n  1026→    Solution:\n  1027→        Refactor your code to use ``==`` instead ``in``.\n  1028→\n  1029→    Example::\n  1030→\n  1031→        # Correct:\n  1032→        a == 's'\n  1033→\n  1034→        # Wrong:\n  1035→        a in {'s'}\n  1036→\n  1037→    .. versionadded:: 0.13.0\n  1038→\n  1039→    \"\"\"\n  1040→\n  1041→    error_template = 'Found wrong `in` compare with single item container'\n  1042→    code = 525\n  1043→\n  1044→\n  1045→@final\n  1046→class ImplicitYieldFromViolation(ASTViolation):\n  1047→    \"\"\"\n  1048→    Forbid ``yield`` inside ``for`` loop instead of ``yield from``.\n  1049→\n  1050→    Reasoning:\n  1051→        It is known that ``yield from`` is a semantically identical\n  1052→        to a ``for`` loop with a ``yield`` inside.\n  1053→        But, it is way more readable.\n  1054→\n  1055→    Solution:\n  1056→        Use ``yield from`` some iterable directly\n  1057→        instead iterating over it inside a loop\n  1058→        and ``yield`` it one by one.\n  1059→\n  1060→    Example::\n  1061→\n  1062→        # Correct:\n  1063→        yield from some()\n  1064→\n  1065→        yield from (\n  1066→            value[index:index + chunk_size]\n  1067→            for index in range(0, len(value), chunk_size)\n  1068→        )\n  1069→\n  1070→        # Wrong:\n  1071→        for index in chunk:\n  1072→            yield index\n  1073→\n  1074→    .. versionadded:: 0.13.0\n  1075→\n  1076→    \"\"\"\n  1077→\n  1078→    error_template = 'Found implicit `yield from` usage'\n  1079→    code = 526\n  1080→\n  1081→\n  1082→@final\n  1083→class NotATupleArgumentViolation(ASTViolation):\n  1084→    \"\"\"\n  1085→    Require tuples as arguments for certain functions.\n  1086→\n  1087→    Reasoning:\n  1088→        For some functions, it is better to use tuples instead of another\n  1089→        iterable types (list, sets,...) as arguments.\n  1090→\n  1091→    Solution:\n  1092→        Use tuples as arguments.\n  1093→\n  1094→    Example::\n  1095→\n  1096→        # Correct:\n  1097→        a = frozenset((2,))\n  1098→\n  1099→        # Wrong:\n  1100→        a = frozenset([2])\n  1101→\n  1102→    See\n  1103→    :py:data:`~wemake_python_styleguide.constants.TUPLE_ARGUMENTS_METHODS`\n  1104→    for full list of methods that we check for.\n  1105→\n  1106→    .. versionadded:: 0.13.0\n  1107→\n  1108→    \"\"\"\n  1109→\n  1110→    error_template = 'Found not a tuple used as an argument'\n  1111→    code = 527\n  1112→\n  1113→\n  1114→@final\n  1115→class ImplicitItemsIteratorViolation(ASTViolation):\n  1116→    \"\"\"\n  1117→    Forbid implicit ``.items()`` iterator.\n  1118→\n  1119→    Reasoning:\n  1120→        When iterating over collection it is easy to forget\n  1121→        to use ``.items()`` when you need to access both keys and values.\n  1122→        So, when you access the iterable with the key inside a ``for`` loop,\n  1123→        that's a sign to refactor your code.\n  1124→\n  1125→    Solution:\n  1126→        Use ``.items()`` with direct keys and values when you need them.\n  1127→\n  1128→    Example::\n  1129→\n  1130→        # Correct:\n  1131→        for some_key, some_value in collection.items():\n  1132→            print(some_key, some_value)\n  1133→\n  1134→        # Wrong:\n  1135→        for some_key in collection:\n  1136→            print(some_key, collection[some_key])\n  1137→\n  1138→    .. versionadded:: 0.13.0\n  1139→\n  1140→    \"\"\"\n  1141→\n  1142→    error_template = 'Found implicit `.items()` usage'\n  1143→    code = 528\n  1144→\n  1145→\n  1146→@final\n  1147→class ImplicitDictGetViolation(ASTViolation):\n  1148→    \"\"\"\n  1149→    Forbid implicit ``.get()`` dict method.\n  1150→\n  1151→    Reasoning:\n  1152→        When using ``in`` with a dict key it is hard to keep the code clean.\n  1153→        It is more convenient to use ``.get()`` and check for ``None`` later.\n  1154→\n  1155→    Solution:\n  1156→        Use ``.get()`` with the key you need.\n  1157→        Check for ``None`` in case you need it,\n  1158→        or just act with the default value of the same type.\n  1159→\n  1160→    Example::\n  1161→\n  1162→        # Correct:\n  1163→        value = collection.get(key)\n  1164→        if value is not None:\n  1165→            print(value)\n  1166→\n  1167→        # Wrong:\n  1168→        if key in collection:\n  1169→            print(collection[key])\n  1170→\n  1171→    .. versionadded:: 0.13.0\n  1172→\n  1173→    \"\"\"\n  1174→\n  1175→    error_template = 'Found implicit `.get()` dict usage'\n  1176→    code = 529\n  1177→\n  1178→\n  1179→@final\n  1180→class ImplicitNegativeIndexViolation(ASTViolation):\n  1181→    \"\"\"\n  1182→    Forbid implicit negative indexes.\n  1183→\n  1184→    Reasoning:\n  1185→        There's no need in getting the length of an iterable\n  1186→        and then having a negative offset,\n  1187→        when you can specify negative indexes in the first place.\n  1188→\n  1189→    Solution:\n  1190→        Use negative indexes.\n  1191→\n  1192→    Example::\n  1193→\n  1194→        # Correct:\n  1195→        some_list[-1]\n  1196→\n  1197→        # Wrong:\n  1198→        some_list[len(some_list) - 1]\n  1199→\n  1200→    .. versionadded:: 0.13.0\n  1201→\n  1202→    \"\"\"\n  1203→\n  1204→    error_template = 'Found implicit negative index'\n  1205→    code = 530\n  1206→\n  1207→\n  1208→@final\n  1209→class SimplifiableReturningIfViolation(ASTViolation):\n  1210→    \"\"\"\n  1211→    Forbid if statements that simply return booleans in functions or methods.\n  1212→\n  1213→    Reasoning:\n  1214→        There is no need to test a condition and simply return a boolean\n  1215→        depending on its outcome if there is not going to be any additional\n  1216→        code.\n  1217→\n  1218→    Solution:\n  1219→        Instead of testing the condition and returning a boolean, return the\n  1220→        condition itself. This applies to early returning ifs too.\n  1221→\n  1222→    Example::\n  1223→\n  1224→        # Correct:\n  1225→        def some_function():\n  1226→            return some_condition\n  1227→\n  1228→        # Wrong:\n  1229→        def some_function():\n  1230→            if some_condition:\n  1231→                return True\n  1232→            else:\n  1233→                return False\n  1234→\n  1235→    .. versionadded:: 0.15.0\n  1236→\n  1237→    \"\"\"\n  1238→\n  1239→    error_template = 'Found simplifiable returning `if` condition in a function'\n  1240→    code = 531\n  1241→\n  1242→\n  1243→@final\n  1244→class ChainedIsViolation(ASTViolation):\n  1245→    \"\"\"\n  1246→    Forbid `ast.Is` in `ast.Compare.ops` when it's size is not zero.\n  1247→\n  1248→    Reasoning:\n  1249→        From the AST perspective, `is` is an operator and can be chained.\n  1250→        That can lead to unexpected results when the author wanted to\n  1251→        compare the result of `a is b` operation. Instead, Python will chain\n  1252→        the operations and compare the last argument of the previous operation.\n  1253→\n  1254→    Solution:\n  1255→        Who knows at this point.\n  1256→\n  1257→    Example::\n  1258→        Correct:\n  1259→            a is b and b is None\n  1260→\n  1261→        Wrong:\n  1262→            a is b is None\n  1263→\n  1264→    .. versionadded:: 0.18.0\n  1265→    \"\"\"\n  1266→\n  1267→    error_template = 'Found chained `is` operators in an expression'\n  1268→    code = 532\n  1269→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0c882c18-b884-456a-b769-9ab57b63089f","uuid":"bbaa0c2e-bbfe-4b3e-ba76-0941b108acee","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/testbed/wemake_python_styleguide/violations/refactoring.py","content":"\"\"\"\nThese checks ensure that you don't have patterns that can be refactored.\n\nThere are so many ways of doing the same thing in Python.\nHere we collect know patterns that can be rewritten into\nmuch easier or just more pythonic version.\n\n.. currentmodule:: wemake_python_styleguide.violations.refactoring\n\nSummary\n-------\n\n.. autosummary::\n   :nosignatures:\n\n   UselessLoopElseViolation\n   UselessFinallyViolation\n   SimplifiableIfViolation\n   UselessReturningElseViolation\n   NegatedConditionsViolation\n   NestedTryViolation\n   UselessLambdaViolation\n   UselessLenCompareViolation\n   NotOperatorWithCompareViolation\n   NestedTernaryViolation\n   WrongInCompareTypeViolation\n   UnmergedIsinstanceCallsViolation\n   WrongIsinstanceWithTupleViolation\n   ImplicitElifViolation\n   ImplicitInConditionViolation\n   OpenWithoutContextManagerViolation\n   TypeCompareViolation\n   PointlessStarredViolation\n   ImplicitEnumerateViolation\n   ImplicitSumViolation\n   FalsyConstantCompareViolation\n   WrongIsCompareViolation\n   ImplicitPrimitiveViolation\n   AlmostSwappedViolation\n   MisrefactoredAssignmentViolation\n   InCompareWithSingleItemContainerViolation\n   ImplicitYieldFromViolation\n   NotATupleArgumentViolation\n   ImplicitItemsIteratorViolation\n   ImplicitDictGetViolation\n   ImplicitNegativeIndexViolation\n   SimplifiableReturningIfViolation\n   ChainedIsViolation\n\nRefactoring opportunities\n-------------------------\n\n.. autoclass:: UselessLoopElseViolation\n.. autoclass:: UselessFinallyViolation\n.. autoclass:: SimplifiableIfViolation\n.. autoclass:: UselessReturningElseViolation\n.. autoclass:: NegatedConditionsViolation\n.. autoclass:: NestedTryViolation\n.. autoclass:: UselessLambdaViolation\n.. autoclass:: UselessLenCompareViolation\n.. autoclass:: NotOperatorWithCompareViolation\n.. autoclass:: NestedTernaryViolation\n.. autoclass:: WrongInCompareTypeViolation\n.. autoclass:: UnmergedIsinstanceCallsViolation\n.. autoclass:: WrongIsinstanceWithTupleViolation\n.. autoclass:: ImplicitElifViolation\n.. autoclass:: ImplicitInConditionViolation\n.. autoclass:: OpenWithoutContextManagerViolation\n.. autoclass:: TypeCompareViolation\n.. autoclass:: PointlessStarredViolation\n.. autoclass:: ImplicitEnumerateViolation\n.. autoclass:: ImplicitSumViolation\n.. autoclass:: FalsyConstantCompareViolation\n.. autoclass:: WrongIsCompareViolation\n.. autoclass:: ImplicitPrimitiveViolation\n.. autoclass:: AlmostSwappedViolation\n.. autoclass:: MisrefactoredAssignmentViolation\n.. autoclass:: InCompareWithSingleItemContainerViolation\n.. autoclass:: ImplicitYieldFromViolation\n.. autoclass:: NotATupleArgumentViolation\n.. autoclass:: ImplicitItemsIteratorViolation\n.. autoclass:: ImplicitDictGetViolation\n.. autoclass:: ImplicitNegativeIndexViolation\n.. autoclass:: SimplifiableReturningIfViolation\n.. autoclass:: ChainedIsViolation\n\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom typing_extensions import final\n\nfrom wemake_python_styleguide.violations.base import (\n    ASTViolation,\n    TokenizeViolation,\n)\n\n\n@final\nclass UselessLoopElseViolation(ASTViolation):\n    \"\"\"\n    Forbid ``else`` without ``break`` in a loop.\n\n    We use the same logic for ``for`` and ``while`` loops.\n\n    Reasoning:\n        When there's no ``break`` keyword in loop's body it means\n        that ``else`` will always be called.\n        This rule will reduce complexity, improve readability,\n        and protect from possible errors.\n\n    Solution:\n        Refactor your ``else`` case logic to be inside the loop's body.\n        Or right after it.\n\n    Example::\n\n        # Correct:\n        for letter in 'abc':\n            if letter == 'b':\n                break\n        else:\n            print('\"b\" is not found')\n\n        for letter in 'abc':\n            print(letter)\n        print('always called')\n\n        # Wrong:\n        for letter in 'abc':\n            print(letter)\n        else:\n            print('always called')\n\n    .. versionadded:: 0.3.0\n    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n\n    \"\"\"\n\n    error_template = 'Found `else` in a loop without `break`'\n    code = 500\n    previous_codes = {436}\n\n\n@final\nclass UselessFinallyViolation(ASTViolation):\n    \"\"\"\n    Forbid ``finally`` in ``try`` block without ``except`` block.\n\n    However, we allow to use ``try`` with just ``finally`` block\n    when function or method is decorated. Because we cannot control\n    what is going on in this decorator.\n    It might be ``@contextmanager`` or similar thing that requires this API.\n\n    Reasoning:\n        This rule will reduce complexity and improve readability.\n\n    Solution:\n        Refactor your ``try`` logic.\n        Replace the ``try-finally`` statement with a ``with`` statement.\n\n    Example::\n\n        # Correct:\n        with open(\"filename\") as f:\n            f.write(...)\n\n        # Wrong:\n        try:\n            f = open(\"filename\")\n            f.write(...)\n        finally:\n            f.close()\n\n    .. versionadded:: 0.3.0\n    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n    .. versionchanged:: 0.14.0\n\n    \"\"\"\n\n    error_template = 'Found `finally` in `try` block without `except`'\n    code = 501\n    previous_codes = {437}\n\n\n@final\nclass SimplifiableIfViolation(ASTViolation):\n    \"\"\"\n    Forbid simplifiable ``if`` conditions.\n\n    Reasoning:\n        These complex constructions can cause frustration among other\n        developers. They are longer, more verbose, and more complex.\n\n    Solution:\n        Either use ``bool()`` to convert test values to boolean values, or just\n        leave it as it is in case your test already returns a boolean value.\n        Use can also use ``not`` keyword to switch boolean values.\n\n    Example::\n\n        # Correct:\n        my_bool = bool(some_call())\n        other_value = 8 if some_call() else None\n\n        # Wrong:\n        my_bool = True if some_call() else False\n\n    We only check ``if`` nodes where ``True`` and ``False`` values are used.\n    We check both ``if`` nodes and ``if`` expressions.\n\n    .. versionadded:: 0.7.0\n    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n\n    \"\"\"\n\n    error_template = 'Found simplifiable `if` condition'\n    code = 502\n    previous_codes = {451}\n\n\n@final\nclass UselessReturningElseViolation(ASTViolation):\n    \"\"\"\n    Forbid useless ``else`` cases in returning functions.\n\n    We check single ``if``, ``for``, ``while``, and ``try``\n    statements that all contain\n    ``return``, ``raise``, ``continue``, or ``break``\n    statements with this rule.\n\n    Reasoning:\n        Using extra ``else`` creates a situation when\n        the whole node could and should be dropped\n        without any changes in logic.\n        So, we prefer to have less code than more code.\n\n    Solution:\n        Remove useless ``else`` case.\n\n    Example::\n\n        # Correct:\n        def some_function():\n            if some_call():\n                return 'yeap'\n            return 'nope'\n\n        # Wrong:\n        def some_function():\n            if some_call():\n                raise ValueError('yeap')\n            else:\n                raise ValueError('nope')\n\n    .. versionadded:: 0.7.0\n    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n    .. versionchanged:: 0.15.1\n\n    \"\"\"\n\n    error_template = 'Found useless returning `else` statement'\n    code = 503\n    previous_codes = {457}\n\n\n@final\nclass NegatedConditionsViolation(ASTViolation):\n    \"\"\"\n    Forbid negated conditions together with ``else`` clause.\n\n    Reasoning:\n        It easier to read and name regular conditions. Not negated ones.\n\n    Solution:\n        Move actions from the negated ``if`` condition to the ``else``\n        condition.\n\n    Example::\n\n        # Correct:\n        if some == 1:\n             ...\n        else:\n             ...\n\n        if not some:\n             ...\n\n        if not some:\n            ...\n        elif other:\n            ...\n\n        # Wrong:\n        if not some:\n             ...\n        else:\n             ...\n\n    .. versionadded:: 0.8.0\n    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n\n    \"\"\"\n\n    error_template = 'Found negated condition'\n    code = 504\n    previous_codes = {463}\n\n\n@final\nclass NestedTryViolation(ASTViolation):\n    \"\"\"\n    Forbid nested ``try`` blocks.\n\n    Notice, we check all possible slots for ``try`` block:\n    1. the ``try`` block itself\n    2. all ``except`` cases\n    3. ``else`` case\n    4. and ``finally`` case\n\n    Reasoning:\n        Nesting ``try`` blocks indicates\n        that something really bad happens to your logic.\n        Why does it require two separate exception handlers?\n        It is a perfect case to refactor your code.\n\n    Solution:\n        Collapse two exception handlers together.\n        Or create a separate function that will handle this second nested case.\n\n    Example::\n\n        # Wrong:\n        try:\n            try:\n                ...\n            except SomeException:\n                ...\n        except SomeOtherException:\n            ...\n\n        try:\n            ...\n        except SomeOtherException:\n            try:\n                ...\n            except SomeException:\n                ...\n\n    .. versionadded:: 0.8.0\n    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n\n    \"\"\"\n\n    error_template = 'Found nested `try` block'\n    code = 505\n    previous_codes = {464}\n\n\n@final\nclass UselessLambdaViolation(ASTViolation):\n    \"\"\"\n    Forbid useless proxy ``lambda`` expressions.\n\n    Reasoning:\n        Sometimes developers tend to overuse ``lambda`` expressions\n        and they wrap code that can be passed as is, without extra wrapping.\n        The code without extra ``lambda`` is easier\n        to read and is more performant.\n\n    Solution:\n        Remove wrapping ``lambda`` declaration, use just the internal function.\n\n    Example::\n\n        # Correct:\n        numbers = map(int, ['1', '2'])\n\n        # Wrong:\n        numbers = map(lambda string: int(string), ['1', '2'])\n\n    .. versionadded:: 0.10.0\n    .. versionchanged:: 0.11.0\n\n    \"\"\"\n\n    error_template = 'Found useless lambda declaration'\n    code = 506\n    previous_codes = {467}\n\n\n@final\nclass UselessLenCompareViolation(ASTViolation):\n    \"\"\"\n    Forbid unpythonic zero-length compare.\n\n    Note, that we allow to check arbitrary length, like ``len(arr) == 3``.\n\n    Reasoning:\n  